/* ================= D1 · Knowledge Hub — direct enquiry, intent-resolved retrieval =================
 *
 * WHY THIS SURFACE EXISTS, IN ONE SENTENCE: a 110-page report was asked for the LATEST
 * value and answered from page 20 rather than page 3, because the retrieval statement
 * orders by COSINE DISTANCE and "latest" is not a similarity question.
 *
 * THE DEFECT IS NOT THAT THE SEARCH WAS BAD. It was correct. A doctor's sentence naming a
 * value explicitly sits far closer in embedding space to "what is the latest X" than a row
 * in a results table does. The top-k was well ordered and wrong for the question asked.
 * A governance layer over the same retrieval would have recorded the wrong answer neatly.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SURFACE DEMONSTRATES, AND WHAT EACH PART IS FOR:
 *
 *   1  INTENT IS RESOLVED BEFORE RETRIEVAL, AND IT PICKS THE ORDERING KEY.
 *      "latest / most recent / current" resolves to LATEST, whose ordering key is the
 *      OBSERVATION DATE carried on the chunk, with similarity as the tie-break. A plain
 *      question resolves to LOOKUP and orders by similarity as it does today. The ordering
 *      is part of CANDIDATE GENERATION, never a re-sort afterwards - LP-08.3 TEST[4] says
 *      Java re-ranks nothing and a re-sort fails "even when it happens to produce the same
 *      order", so the intent has to reach the statement, not the caller.
 *
 *   2  OBSERVATION DATE IS NOT THE AS-OF DATE. The estate's :as_of is DOCUMENT VALIDITY -
 *      when a document was in force. This is a different axis: when the FACT INSIDE it was
 *      observed. A report issued 2026-09-01 can carry a reading taken 2026-08-18. Both are
 *      needed and neither substitutes for the other. In the built system this rides
 *      document_chunk.metadata (jsonb, "flat metadata contract", already on the table) so it
 *      is not a Part-M column delta on a ring-fenced baseline table.
 *
 *   3  THE ANSWER SHOWS WHAT IT DID NOT CHOOSE. The original failure was invisible because
 *      the caller saw one answer and a hedge. Rendering the rejected candidates beside the
 *      chosen one - with date, page, chunk kind and the reason it lost - makes a wrong
 *      ordering legible in one glance instead of after a complaint.
 *
 *   4  TWO NUMBERS, TWO JOBS, NEVER MIXED.
 *        - the ELIGIBILITY FLOOR is on retrieval distance. It GATES: below it a chunk is
 *          not a candidate at all, and when nothing clears it the answer is an EMPTY MARKED
 *          result reporting the floor, never a silent widening (LP-08.3 TEST[6], and the
 *          reporting half is RES-15-A, which today "does not report the floor to the caller,
 *          so a caller cannot tell a confident empty from a thin one").
 *        - CONFIDENCE is a SIGNAL and gates nothing. governance_constraints[4], asserted at
 *          LP-07.1 TEST[3] and LP-07.2 TEST[5]: no statement filters, ranks or thresholds on
 *          it. It is composed of parts that are shown, so a low number can be ARGUED WITH
 *          rather than merely distrusted.
 *
 *   5  THE AUTHORITATIVE MARKER IS DERIVED, NOT CONSTANT (DD-KH-1). Four lanes assert that a
 *      caller cannot receive Hub context without receiving the mark (LP-08.2 TEST[0],
 *      LP-08.3 TEST[5], LP-08.6 TEST[4], LP-08.tests_must_cover[3]). Making the Hub
 *      authoritative does not delete that assertion - it makes the mark's VALUE depend on the
 *      document's governance state, which keeps all four tests true verbatim and makes them
 *      stronger, because a derived mark can now be WRONG and a test can catch it.
 *
 * WHAT THIS SURFACE IS NOT. It decides no policy: the floor, the approver rule and the
 * retirement authority are OPA's, and this screen would render their refusals verbatim. It
 * is a PROTOTYPE of behaviour - the scoring below is seeded, not computed, and says so.
 * =============================================================================== */

/* GOVERNANCE STATE -> AUTHORITATIVE. The lifecycle is a SEPARATE vocabulary from the
   ingestion pipeline (RECEIVED..AVAILABLE): a document is legitimately AVAILABLE and
   PENDING_APPROVAL at once. Folding the two into one enum is B1's exact defect - two
   lifecycles under one type name - so they stay apart here and, in D5, in the schema. */
const KH_GOV_STATE = {
  APPROVED:         { tone: "green", authoritative: true,  why: null },
  PENDING_APPROVAL: { tone: "amber", authoritative: false, why: "Awaiting independent approval — content is retrievable but is not yet authoritative." },
  DRAFT:            { tone: "gray",  authoritative: false, why: "Draft version — not submitted for approval." },
  SUPERSEDED:       { tone: "gray",  authoritative: false, why: "Superseded by a later version — retrievable only at an as-of date when it was live." },
  RETIRED:          { tone: "red",   authoritative: false, why: "Retired — withdrawn by its owner." },
};

